# Copy to apps/api/.env (gitignored):
#   cp apps/api/.env.example apps/api/.env
#
# The credentials here must match infra/.env, which is what actually
# initialises the Postgres container. Full local setup from a fresh clone:
#
#   1. cp infra/.env.example infra/.env       # then edit POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#   2. cp apps/api/.env.example apps/api/.env # set the same password below
#   3. docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.yml up -d
#   4. cd apps/api && bun run db:migrate
#   5. bun test                               # also works from the repo root
#
# If you change POSTGRES_PASSWORD after the volume already exists, Postgres
# will NOT pick it up — credentials are only applied on first initialisation.
# Recreate the volume (destroys local data) with:
#   docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.yml down -v
DATABASE_URL=postgres://diudara:change_me@localhost:5432/diudara
PORT=3000

# REQUIRED, and not optional in the way it looks. Nothing else in this
# repository sets NODE_ENV — Bun does not default it, there is no `start`
# script, no Dockerfile, and no API service in infra/docker-compose.yml — so
# without this line `process.env.NODE_ENV` is `undefined`.
#
# bootstrap() treats NODE_ENV as an ALLOWLIST: the fake payment adapter and an
# absent XENDIT_CALLBACK_TOKEN are permitted ONLY when it is exactly
# "development" or "test" (`bun test` sets "test" itself). Every other value —
# including unset, "staging", "prod" and "PRODUCTION" — either refuses to
# start (messaging: FONNTE_API_TOKEN) or, for Xendit
# specifically, DISABLES payments rather than refusing to start — see the
# Xendit block below. That is deliberate: an unrecognised value must fail
# CLOSED rather than silently taking fake money.
#
# On a real deployment, set the real Xendit variables below to accept paid
# memberships; do NOT set NODE_ENV=development there to make a messaging
# error go away.
NODE_ENV=development

# Signing secret for session JWTs. Generate your own:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# The API refuses to start if this is unset, shorter than 32 characters, or
# still the placeholder value below — every signed-in session depends on this
# one key, and a weak HS256 secret is brute-forceable offline from a single
# token. It signed TWO audiences until retire-telegram Task 7's fix round
# deleted the creator login; the surviving user tokens still carry the
# `typ: "user"` claim that told them apart, because this secret may one day
# sign something that is not a session again.
JWT_SECRET=change_me_to_a_long_random_string

# Xendit. Unset selects the fake payment adapter, which is permitted ONLY when
# NODE_ENV is exactly development or test (all tests rely on it). For every
# other NODE_ENV — including unset — leaving BOTH unset now DISABLES payments
# rather than refusing to start: `bootstrap()` boots with no payment provider
# at all, and every route that would need one answers 503
# ("pembayaran belum dikonfigurasi di server ini.") rather than 500ing or
# minting a fake invoice. There are two, and they are the whole money surface
# of this box:
#
#   POST /users/me/payout          — connect a person's payout account
#   POST /users/:handle/subscribe  — buy a membership
#
# (There were three. `POST /payment-account`, the OLD creator world's Xendit
# onboarding, went with `POST /auth/*` in retire-telegram Task 7's fix round:
# the dashboard that was its only caller had been deleted six tasks earlier.)
#
# The read-only companion (GET /users/me/payout) still answers normally with
# "not connected", so a screen can tell "press the button" apart from "this
# server cannot take payments at all".
#
# Retire-telegram Task 4 deleted what this paragraph used to describe: a fourth
# route, POST /c/:slug/checkout, which was not REGISTERED at all in this case,
# and the `access_mode = "request"` instruction for communities that could not
# be sold. Communities are gone; there is nothing for an operator to set.
#
# Partial configuration (one set, the other unset) still THROWS in EVERY
# environment, unchanged: it is never intentional, and it makes an operator
# believe payments are live. Once BOTH are set, real money moves and a
# misconfigured box must not silently take fake payments and write
# fake-acct-* ids into app_user.xendit_account_id, which cannot be undone
# without manual SQL — that is still what the fake adapter's own
# development/test-only restriction guards against.
# XENDIT_SECRET_KEY=
# XENDIT_SPLIT_RULE_ID=
# The static token Xendit sends as the X-CALLBACK-TOKEN header on every webhook.
# It is the ONLY thing authenticating POST /webhooks/xendit — Xendit signs nothing.
# Same thresholds as the two variables above: unset is fine when NODE_ENV is
# development or test (the webhook route then rejects every delivery, so set it if
# you want to exercise that path locally), AND — mirroring the Xendit keys
# themselves — unset is now also fine for any other NODE_ENV, PROVIDED
# XENDIT_SECRET_KEY/XENDIT_SPLIT_RULE_ID are also both unset (payments
# disabled: no invoice will ever exist for this webhook to authenticate).
# bootstrap() still THROWS for any other NODE_ENV if the two keys above ARE
# set without this one — real invoices with no way to authenticate the
# callback means nobody who pays is ever activated. It must also be at least
# 32 characters, exactly like JWT_SECRET above and for the same reason. Copy
# the value from Settings → Developers → Webhooks in the Xendit dashboard.
# The token the test suite uses is committed to this repository, so it is
# rejected here too.
# XENDIT_CALLBACK_TOKEN=

# The messaging provider — how a person is actually reached.
#
# FONNTE_API_TOKEN is what FonnteWhatsAppAdapter uses to send WhatsApp messages:
# password-reset links, signup notices, membership reminders. WhatsApp is
# NOTIFICATION-ONLY: it cannot gate access to anything, and FonnteWhatsAppAdapter
# throws rather than pretending otherwise — see the WHY NOTIFY ONLY note in its
# source.
#
# Retire-telegram Task 2 removed TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN from this block along with
# TelegramBotAdapter and everything it gated. There is no second messaging token
# to be half-configured against any more.
#
# Same rule as the Xendit variables above, and for the same reason: leaving it
# unset selects FakeMessagingAdapter, which records sends instead of making them,
# and that is permitted ONLY when NODE_ENV is exactly development or test. For
# every other value — INCLUDING unset, "staging", "prod" and "PRODUCTION" — the
# selector THROWS, because a box that looks like it is messaging paying members
# and is only writing to an array is the worst failure mode here: the member
# appears told and is not.
#
# BOTH processes select it, and both refuse to start without it outside the
# allowlist. It is a bearer credential, so the adapter never puts a URL, a
# request, or a response body into an error message.
# FONNTE_API_TOKEN=

# How often the outbox worker (apps/worker) looks for rows to send, in
# milliseconds. NOTHING WRITES `outbox` ROWS ANY MORE — retire-telegram removed
# the last writer with the Telegram invite it queued, so this interval no longer
# delays anything a member is waiting for. The pass is kept only so a row an
# older deploy left behind fails loudly rather than sitting `pending` and unread,
# and it retires with the `outbox` table (see `bootstrapWorker`), taking this
# variable with it. Until then: every tick is one indexed query, so smaller is
# not free. Unset means 5000. A value that is not a positive whole number THROWS
# rather than defaulting: Number("abc") is NaN, and setTimeout treats NaN as 0 —
# a worker hammering the database instead of polling it.
# WORKER_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=5000

# How often the SAME worker runs its FIVE scheduled passes, in milliseconds:
# the orphan-media sweep, the expired-membership sweep, the membership reminder,
# the stale-pending-checkout cleanup, and the user-stream sweep. Unset means
# 3600000 (one hour), and it is deliberately three orders of magnitude longer
# than the outbox interval above: none of the five is latency-sensitive the way
# somebody waiting on a payment is, and in every case it is the pass's OWN window
# — 24 hours for orphan media, two for a stale checkout, twelve for a user
# stream, three days for the reminder — rather than this cadence that protects
# what it sweeps, so an hourly wake-up closes any of their gaps within about an
# hour of it opening. It is not 24 hours either — the loop measures from the
# previous pass, not from a time of day, so a daily interval would pin every
# member's reminder to whatever time the worker last restarted.
#
# THE NAME IS PHASE 5'S, AND IS KEPT ON PURPOSE. Retire-telegram Task 4 deleted
# the renewal and churn passes this interval was chosen for, and with them
# `renewal_reminder`'s per-stage claim; the surviving warning is
# RemindExpiringMembership over `membership_reminder`. The five passes above
# shared that cadence and stayed, so the knob stayed with them rather than being
# renamed: an env var is a deployment's contract, and renaming it would silently
# reset every box that sets it back to the default. See
# DEFAULT_RENEWAL_INTERVAL_MS in apps/worker/src/scheduled-passes.ts. Same
# fail-closed parsing as above.
# WORKER_RENEWAL_INTERVAL_MS=3600000

# Live streaming (MediaMTX). Unlike Xendit/Fonnte above, an absent
# configuration here does NOT block boot — the product works fine without
# streaming (design spec §7): absence selects FakeStreamingAdapter in development/test
# (same RELAXED_NODE_ENVS allowlist as everywhere else) and in every other
# NODE_ENV the API boots normally with the creator's streaming UI hidden
# rather than offering a "go live" button that always fails.
#
# All FIVE of the variables below are set together or not at all. Partial
# configuration (any one to four set, but not all five) THROWS in EVERY
# environment — a host with no webhook secret is a webhook nothing can
# authenticate, and a webhook secret with no host is a signing key naming a
# stream nobody can reach.
#
# EVERY PATH BELOW CARRIES THE `u/` NAMESPACE. Retire-telegram Task 6 left
# `parseStreamPath` with exactly one namespace — `u`, a person's own broadcast —
# and Task 7 deleted the community `/live/` locations from nginx entirely. An
# operator who copies a `/live/...` URL from an older copy of this file cannot
# go live: MediaMTX is published to at `u/<stream key>` and nothing serves
# `/live/` any more. The three shapes are built in one place,
# MediaMTxStreamingAdapter.createSession (apps/api/src/infrastructure/streaming/
# mediamtx.adapter.ts), and two tests pin them.
#
# MEDIAMTX_RTMP_HOST is what a creator's OBS is told to publish to:
#   rtmp://<MEDIAMTX_RTMP_HOST>:1935/u/<stream key>
# MEDIAMTX_HLS_BASE_URL is what a member's browser is told to play from — the
# public https:// origin nginx proxies to MediaMTX's HLS port (which itself
# stays bound to 127.0.0.1; see infra/mediamtx.yml, Task 6):
#   <MEDIAMTX_HLS_BASE_URL>/u/<stream key>/index.m3u8
# No trailing slash needed; one is stripped if present.
# MEDIAMTX_WHIP_BASE_URL (Task 2) is what a creator's BROWSER is told to
# publish to, as a WebRTC alternative to OBS's RTMP above:
#   <MEDIAMTX_WHIP_BASE_URL>/whip/u/<stream key>
# The `/whip/` sits IN FRONT of the namespace rather than inside it, so this
# shape does not parallel the HLS one: nginx serves `^~ /whip/u/` as its own
# top-level prefix location. That is deliberate and it is not stylistic. The
# read path is a `^~ /u/` PREFIX location, and per nginx's own
# location-selection algorithm a `^~` prefix location, once chosen as the
# longest match, makes nginx skip EVERY regex location for that request — so a
# `~ ^/u/(.+)/whip$` location placed anywhere in the file, in any order, would
# never be reached, and publishing would be swallowed by the read location's
# `auth_request`. A prefix that does not start with `/u/` is the only way to
# serve WHIP without touching that security-critical logic. Verified against a
# real MediaMTX instance and a real publish (Task 1) before this adapter was
# written. No trailing slash needed; one is stripped if present.
# MEDIAMTX_WHIP_BASE_URL and MEDIAMTX_HLS_BASE_URL must be the SAME public
# origin — nginx serves both `^~ /u/` and `^~ /whip/u/` off one server block
# (infra/nginx/live-hls.conf.template) — but nothing checks that for you; they
# are two independent strings a deploy can set inconsistently with no error
# until a creator tries browser publishing.
# MEDIAMTX_WEBHOOK_SECRET authenticates BOTH MediaMTX webhooks (Task 4) —
# the ONLY thing standing in for Xendit's X-CALLBACK-TOKEN on this pair of
# endpoints. STREAM_TOKEN_SECRET signs every watch token
# (apps/api/src/domain/user-watch-token.ts) and must NEVER be the same value as
# JWT_SECRET: different audience, different lifetime, and a compromise of
# one must not compromise the other.
# Both secrets must be at least 32 characters, exactly like JWT_SECRET above
# and for the same reason — each is the only thing standing between a
# stranger and a paid stream. Generate them separately:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# MEDIAMTX_RTMP_HOST=
# MEDIAMTX_HLS_BASE_URL=
# MEDIAMTX_WHIP_BASE_URL=
# MEDIAMTX_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# STREAM_TOKEN_SECRET=

# Email (Task 4, Resend). Unlike Xendit/Fonnte above, an absent
# configuration here does NOT block boot — the SAME divergence
# selectPaymentProvider makes for the reason free communities gave it: Task
# 5's password reset falls back to WhatsApp when a user has a number and
# messaging is configured, and sends nothing when neither channel is
# available, so refusing to start over a missing email provider would let an
# OPTIONAL channel take down account recovery entirely.
#
# Absence selects FakeEmailAdapter in development/test (same RELAXED_NODE_ENVS
# allowlist as everywhere else — the fake records sends into an array instead
# of making them). In every other NODE_ENV — including unset, "staging" and
# "PRODUCTION" — absence DISABLES email rather than refusing to start:
# selectEmailProvider returns null, never the fake adapter, so Task 5 can see
# there is no email channel and try WhatsApp instead of silently believing a
# send happened.
#
# Partial configuration (one set, the other unset) still THROWS in EVERY
# environment, unchanged from every other provider pair in this file: it is
# never intentional, and it makes an operator believe email is live.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY is a bearer credential (sent as "Authorization: Bearer
# <key>") — same handling rule as FONNTE_API_TOKEN
# above: ResendEmailAdapter never puts it, the request, or the raw response
# body into a thrown error message. EMAIL_FROM is the "From" address/name
# Resend sends as, e.g. "DIUDARA <no-reply@yourdomain.example>" — it must be
# a domain verified in your Resend dashboard.
#
# ResendEmailAdapter is UNVERIFIED against the live API — see the warning
# comment at the top of resend-email.adapter.ts. There is no key anywhere in
# this repository's history to test one against.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=

# Image storage (Phase 4, Task 2) — Biznet Gio NEO Object Storage, or any
# other S3-compatible bucket. Get the five values below from the Biznet Gio
# NEO portal's Access page (Object Storage -> your bucket -> Access), which
# issues the access key ID/secret pair and shows the bucket's own endpoint
# and region.
#
# UNLIKE every other absence-tolerant provider in this file (Xendit above is
# the one exception, and streaming/AI/email are the graceful-degradation
# ones), a missing bucket here BLOCKS BOOT outside NODE_ENV=development/test,
# the same allowlist as everywhere else. That is a deliberate asymmetry: an
# API that accepts image uploads and quietly keeps them in a Map that vanishes
# on the next restart or deploy is worse than one that refuses to start —
# nothing tells a creator their post's photo is gone until a follower reports
# a broken image. Absence selects FakeMediaStorageAdapter ONLY in
# development/test (the fake keeps bytes in memory instead of a bucket, which
# is fine for a process that is expected to restart constantly and never
# needs the bytes to survive that).
#
# All FIVE variables are set together or not at all — partial configuration
# THROWS in EVERY environment, same reasoning as every other provider pair in
# this file: an access key with no bucket is never intentional.
#
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID and S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY are bearer credentials — same
# handling rule as RESEND_API_KEY above:
# S3MediaStorageAdapter never puts them, or a request, or a raw response body,
# into a thrown error message. S3_BUCKET is the bucket name. S3_ENDPOINT is
# the S3-compatible endpoint (Biznet Gio NEO's is region-specific, e.g.
# https://s3.id-jkt-1.neo.id) — this adapter always passes an explicit
# endpoint, never AWS's default, because Biznet Gio NEO is not AWS.
# S3_REGION is the region id (e.g. id-jkt-1).
#
# The key layout (posts/<id>/full.webp, posts/<id>/thumb.webp) lives ONLY
# inside s3-media-storage.adapter.ts — nothing else in this codebase composes
# a bucket key or a URL, which is what keeps a bucket URL from ever escaping
# into an HTTP response (design spec §5.1, Phase 6's paywall depends on it).
#
# S3MediaStorageAdapter is UNVERIFIED against a live bucket — see the warning
# comment at the top of s3-media-storage.adapter.ts. There is no bucket
# anywhere in this repository's history to test one against.
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ENDPOINT=
# S3_REGION=

# Most images a single post may carry (Phase 4, Task 7 of images). Unset
# means 5. A RUNTIME env var rather than a shared constant, deliberately
# (images design spec §6): the web is a static build served by nginx and
# cannot read this process's env, so it fetches GET /users/limits once at
# boot to learn this number instead — that endpoint is advisory only, and
# this value stays the sole authority, enforced server-side on both
# POST /users/posts and PATCH /users/posts/:id.
#
# A value that is not a whole number of at least 1 THROWS rather than
# defaulting, same fail-closed parsing as WORKER_POLL_INTERVAL_MS above:
# Number("abc") is NaN, and a cap silently coerced to NaN would reject every
# post carrying an image, not "no cap".
# MAX_POST_IMAGES=5

# Base URL of the PUBLIC web app (apps/web). Every link this API hands to a
# person or to a third party is built from it, and there are exactly three:
#
#   <APP_BASE_URL>/@<handle>      StartUserSubscription's success_redirect_url —
#                                 where Xendit sends the payer after paying
#   <APP_BASE_URL>/@<handle>      RemindExpiringMembership's "buy again" link
#   <APP_BASE_URL>/reset/<token>  RequestPasswordReset's emailed reset link
#
# Without it the payer is left on Xendit's own receipt with no way back, and a
# password reset points at whichever host the API happened to think it was.
# Retire-telegram Task 4 deleted the fourth shape this line used to name,
# <APP_BASE_URL>/c/<slug>/status/<subscriptionId>: the community checkout's
# confirmation page. Nothing builds it any more.
#
# The WORKER resolves this variable through the same resolver, for the reminder
# link above — see apps/api/src/worker-bootstrap.ts. Defaults to the Vite dev
# server below; set it to the real https:// origin on a deployment. No trailing
# slash.
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173
